fields.Core Themes JSON:
[
  {
    "title": "A Truth Bomb In Verse",
    "body": "Chance opens in rhyme before handing the introduction to Brandon Thomas, newly minted co host of Mysterious Universe alongside Joe Hodge, who took that show over from Ben and Aaron after nineteen years. The occasion is Tamara Sheville's book Hacking Hoaxes: Nuclear Weapons Truth Bomb and Explosion of Lies, written entirely in rhyming verse, which Thomas had already covered on his show as A New Clear Perspective. All three linger on the synchronicity, since Chance was emailing Sheville the very day he first spoke with Thomas by phone, and Chance frames his opening question around Iran, suggesting nukes work as an excuse for war rather than a deterrent."
  },
  {
    "title": "Inside The Hazmat Machine",
    "body": "Sheville traces her path from volunteering at a fire department and taking hazardous materials classes at the National Fire Academy to work under the Nunn Lugar Domenici Act, which trained the 120 most populated cities through tabletop and full scale chemical and biological exercises. She sat on the NFPA hazmat committee, served as curriculum coordinator for terrorism training at the Department of Justice before it joined Homeland Security, worked the Metropolitan Medical Strike Team, and handled live Sarin nerve agent at Fort McClellan. Her husband is a retired Green Beret who later worked at the Nevada Test Site, and hearing David Weiss say nukes are fake struck at her identity."
  },
  {
    "title": "God Replaced By Government",
    "body": "Chance argues that for most of history a priest class warned of imminent destruction by flood or by fire from heaven unless people obeyed the intermediaries, and that once a secular society swapped God for government the same leverage required giving government the power to end the world. He calls the nuclear football a Hollywood MacGuffin, like the briefcase in Pulp Fiction or Kramer's crackers in Seinfeld, an object a plot revolves around whose contents are never shown. Sheville presses the point that Little Boy supposedly worked the first time, untested, and Thomas answers with the first crewed moon landing going perfectly on the first try."
  },
  {
    "title": "What The Pilot Never Saw",
    "body": "The evidentiary core rests on Alexander de Seversky, the Russian pilot then flying for the American military, who had already surveyed Dresden, St Petersburg and 62 firebombed Japanese cities and who, Sheville says, walked Hiroshima for three days without finding a hypocenter or any of the famous shadows. She cites Michael Palmer's Hiroshima Revisited, which contends napalm and mustard blistering agent produced the injuries, alongside Thomas Judge's Oppenheimer's Deadly Toys and a declassified interim committee transcript dated May 31, 1945 in which officials concede they lacked enough uranium. She adds that the plutonium in the soil was the wrong fuel, and that Yamaguchi rode a working train between both cities."
  },
  {
    "title": "The Demon Core And Cooling Pools",
    "body": "Sheville walks through subcritical, critical and supercritical states, contending that a reactor is shielded, moderated and cooled so that fission yields roughly 94 percent heat and 6 percent radiation, and that Three Mile Island, Chernobyl and Fukushima overheated rather than exploded. She recounts the two demon core deaths at Los Alamos, one in August 1945 involving dropped tungsten bricks and one involving a slipped screwdriver, each producing a blue flash and no blast. Chance offers a microwave and a sunburn as analogies for radiation that does not linger, Sheville invokes Galen Windsor swimming in cooling pools, and Thomas closes on wearing the label critical thinker."
  }
]

